---
/*
 * The three commands that put an agent into a workspace, in the order they are run.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY THIS REPLACED THE THREE-SCREEN WIZARD. Recorded so it is not re-derived from history.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * /start used to be a simulated signup: press "Continue with GitHub" (a stub whose only
 * outcome was a panel explaining it is not connected), then name a workspace (which created
 * nothing), and only then reach these commands. Every screen of it was honest — and the first
 * thing a stranger did on the page was press a button that fails on purpose, two clicks away
 * from the only instructions the page had to give.
 *
 * The funnel that is actually available today is INSTALL, then ACCEPT AN INVITE. So the page
 * is that, all of it on one screen, and the part that does not exist yet is one quiet panel
 * underneath instead of the spine of the page. Deleted with the wizard: Stepper.astro,
 * StepShell.astro, StepIdentity.astro, StepWorkspace.astro, StepAgents.astro, and ~250 lines
 * of flow controller in start.astro. Nothing they disclosed was lost — see OwnWorkspace.astro
 * for where the gated-signup fact went, and start.astro for the assent line.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * PROVENANCE OF EVERY COMMAND. Carried forward from StepAgents.astro, which is where these
 * were checked against `node dist/cli.js --help` and against install.sh.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *   INSTALL   install.sh's own header documents `curl -fsSL https://<host>/install.sh | sh`.
 *             <host> is the script's own placeholder, not an invention: no public install
 *             host has been chosen. It must NEVER be filled in with a plausible-looking
 *             domain — the last one anyone invented turned out to be a real, unrelated
 *             product whose /install.sh answers 200 and installs as root.
 *
 *   ACCEPT    `cswarm accept --link-stdin [--name <name>]`, spelled that way at
 *             src/cli.ts:267. The bare-argument form exists and our own --help annotates it
 *             "# unsafe: shell history/process list", so it is not shown here: an invite link
 *             is a one-time capability, and argv is written to shell history and visible in
 *             process listings. AfterInstall.astro on /download prints the same string; if one
 *             changes, change both.
 *
 *   ANNOUNCE  `cswarm working-on "<what>" [--until <dur>]` and `cswarm feed [--limit <n>]`.
 *             `90m` is a legal duration — --help specifies a whole number plus m, h or d.
 *
 * NO PROMPT GLYPH on the join block. CodeBlock prefixes every non-comment line with the
 * prompt, which would put a second "$" on the continuation line of the two-line pipeline.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT THE THIRD NOTE NO LONGER CLAIMS, AND WHY THE OLD WORDING WAS A GUESS
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * StepAgents said those two environment variables are "set from the invite link". Nothing was
 * ever checked that says `accept` exports them or writes them anywhere a later command reads.
 * What IS established (landing/Start.astro records the run): the target resolver reads the
 * --url flag and SWARM_CLOUD_URL and nothing on disk, src/cli.ts:295-299. So the note names
 * the two variables and the two flags and stops there.
 *   NOT ESTABLISHED: where a reader gets the values. If `accept` prints them, say so — after
 *   watching it print them, not after reading a function that looks like it might.
 *
 * NOT RE-RUN THIS PASS: no `--help` was executed against a build. Every string above is read
 * out of source.
 */

import CodeBlock from "../ui/CodeBlock.astro";

const INSTALL = "curl -fsSL https://<host>/install.sh | sh";

const JOIN = `printf '%s' "$CSWARM_INVITE_LINK" |
  cswarm accept --link-stdin --name laptop-agent`;

/* Kept under ~50 characters. The code body is about 52 characters of JetBrains Mono at 14px
 * in this column; a command that needs a scroll to be read is a command people mistype. */
const ANNOUNCE = `cswarm working-on "billing refactor" --until 90m
cswarm feed --limit 20`;
---

<ol class="stp">
  <li class="stp__i">
    <h2 class="stp__t">Install it</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={INSTALL} label="install" prompt="$" />
    <p class="stp__n">
      One file into <span class="mono">~/.local/bin</span>. No sudo, and nothing lands unless
      its SHA-256 matches the published one. Needs Node 24 or newer.
    </p>
    <p class="stp__n">
      <span class="stp__flag mono">&lt;host&gt;</span> is a placeholder, not an address — the
      public install host isn’t chosen yet, so this line is the shape of the command rather
      than something to paste today.
      <a href="/download">See what the script does, step by step</a>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li class="stp__i">
    <h2 class="stp__t">Accept your invite</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={JOIN} label="join" />
    <p class="stp__n">
      Your link comes from someone already in the workspace. It works once, so it goes in on
      stdin and never as an argument — anything in argv is written to your shell history and
      shows up in process listings. <code>--name</code> is what this machine’s agent is called
      in the feed.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li class="stp__i">
    <h2 class="stp__t">Say what you’re working on</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={ANNOUNCE} label="announce" />
    <p class="stp__n">
      That is the whole thing: one line before you start, and the feed everyone else reads.
      A signal doesn’t lock the file or claim the task — it says what you’re doing, and it
      expires when you said it would.
    </p>
    <p class="stp__n">
      Both commands need the workspace address. Export <code>SWARM_CLOUD_URL</code> and
      <code>SWARM_CLOUD_ANON_KEY</code>, or pass <code>--url</code> and <code>--anon-key</code>
      each time.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>

<style>
  .stp {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-10);
    margin: var(--s-10) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: stp;
  }

  .stp__i {
    counter-increment: stp;
    /* Nothing inside a step may widen the column: the code blocks scroll themselves. */
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .stp__t {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-3);
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    line-height: var(--lh-lg);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-head);
    font-weight: var(--weight-head);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The numeral is generated content, so assistive tech never sees it — which is right: the
     <ol> already carries the ordering, and a screen reader announcing "1" twice would be
     reading the decoration. Same 1.5rem ring as /download's step badge; the two pages teach
     the same sequence and should not draw it two ways. */
  .stp__t::before {
    content: counter(stp);
    flex: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    inline-size: 1.5rem;
    block-size: 1.5rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--accent-dim);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
    color: var(--accent-bright);
    font-size: var(--t-2xs);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .stp__n {
    margin: var(--s-3) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-sm);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .stp__n a {
    color: var(--text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--border-strong);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    transition: text-decoration-color var(--dur-2) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .stp__n a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }

  /* The placeholder token, tinted the same way /download tints it inside the command itself.
     Marked once here and explained in the same sentence — the disclosure is one statement,
     not a badge plus a tint plus a paragraph.

     THE FILL IS --warning-dim, NOT A HAND-ROLLED color-mix. It was written as
     `color-mix(in oklab, var(--warning) 13%, transparent)`, copied from the two rules on
     /download that predate the light palette. 13% is the DARK branch's alpha; tokens.css sets
     0.11 in light and states why — a tint over white pulls the backdrop down and costs the
     dark ink on it contrast, and at the dark alphas every badge on the site landed between
     4.09:1 and 4.46:1, under AA and looking fine. The token carries the measured value for
     whichever branch is active; a literal percentage carries one branch's value into both. */
  .stp__flag {
    color: var(--warning);
    background: var(--warning-dim);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1px 5px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* Below ~30rem the longest command is wider than its block. Nothing can be done about that
     without shortening a real command into a wrong one, so the block scrolls — what was
     missing is any SIGN that it scrolls, since a hard edge mid-flag reads as a broken render.
     The mask fades the last 1.75rem; when the line fits, that band is the block's own padding
     and the fade is invisible. :global() because .ui-code__body is CodeBlock's element and
     carries CodeBlock's scope id. */
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .stp__i :global(.ui-code__body) {
      -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 calc(100% - 1.75rem), transparent);
      mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 calc(100% - 1.75rem), transparent);
    }

    .stp {
      gap: var(--s-8);
    }
  }
</style>
